          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 345
        },
        {
          "id": "claims",
          "label": "Claims",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 15
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Client-Side Execution Is Real",
        "definition": "Twilite already has a working, sandboxed script-execution facility with a real mutation API and a real (if imperfect) data-flow engine — not a stub, not aspirational documentation. All of it requires an open browser tab to run.",
        "notes": "Caveat found in the research: graphExecutionRuntime.js's resolveNodeOutput() reads node.data synchronously at the moment the topo-walk visits a node, while node execution (ScriptRunner, fetch) is asynchronous — downstream nodes in the same executeGraph() pass likely receive the previous cached result, not a freshly awaited one. This looks more like a snapshot/pull model layered on an event-push mechanism than n8n's true await-then-forward per-run data flow.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-concept",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node",
