    {
      "id": "twilite-automation-readiness-concept-server-trigger",
      "type": "topic-concept",
      "label": "No Server-Side Trigger Surface Exists",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 320
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 380,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "mechanism",
          "label": "Mechanism",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 345
        },
        {
          "id": "claims",
          "label": "Claims",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 15
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "No Server-Side Trigger Surface Exists",
        "definition": "Every trigger-capable node type found (timer, valueTrigger, event-handler) fires only while its graph is open in a live browser tab. There is no webhook receiver, no cron/scheduled job, and no generic 'run this script' server endpoint anywhere in app/api/.",
        "documentation": "grep across components/ found zero webhook/cron/schedule node types. app/api/ routes are entirely account/integration plumbing: GitHub OAuth, the GitHub App marketplace webhook (billing sync, not a Twilite automation hook), Stripe billing, account auth, content, and template-gallery — none accept an arbitrary automation payload or are invoked by a scheduler.",
        "examples": "n8n's core trigger types — a webhook that fires on an external HTTP call, a cron schedule that runs at 3am with nobody watching — have no Twilite equivalent today. TimerNode.js uses a browser setInterval, which stops the moment the tab closes.",
        "notes": "This is the single most consequential gap for 'n8n-style': n8n's core value proposition is specifically that it runs unattended. Nothing found here does.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "twilite-automation-readiness-claim-server-trigger",
      "type": "topic-claim",
      "label": "Unattended Execution Is the Real Missing Piece, Not Scripting Capability",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 840
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 380,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "Concept",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        },
        {
          "id": "perspective",
          "label": "Perspective",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "source",
          "label": "Source",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 90
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Unattended Execution Is the Real Missing Piece, Not Scripting Capability",
        "claim": "Twilite is not 'up to the task' of full n8n-style automation today, but the gap is narrower and more specific than 'no execution engine' — it's specifically the absence of any server-side trigger surface (webhook, cron, or otherwise), not a lack of script-execution or mutation capability.",
        "rationale": "This resolves the open question on the n8n-style-automation-template idea graph more precisely than 'modeled vs executable': it's executable while a tab is open, not executable when it isn't. The fix is a scoped one (a server-side trigger endpoint), not a full new execution engine.",
        "evidence": "See the No Server-Side Trigger Surface Exists concept and its sources.",
        "caveats": "The agent-authored-transaction path (MCP) can't fill this gap as-is either: its propose_* tools never auto-apply (approvalRequired is always true per the MCP README), so even an external agent hitting Twilite today can't autonomously trigger a real mutation, only propose one for a human to accept.",
        "documentation": "See the No Server-Side Trigger Surface Exists concept.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim"
      }
    },
